        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="7OVz4w7n9kzEPWy-4BBC-9" value="6" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#007CBD;strokeColor=none;fontColor=#FFFFFF;fontStyle=1;fontSize=22;labelBackgroundColor=none;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-390" y="2085.88" width="40" height="38" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="7OVz4w7n9kzEPWy-4BBC-10" value="7" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#007CBD;strokeColor=none;fontColor=#FFFFFF;fontStyle=1;fontSize=22;labelBackgroundColor=none;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-385.5" y="2260" width="40" height="38" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="7OVz4w7n9kzEPWy-4BBC-11" value="8" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#007CBD;strokeColor=none;fontColor=#FFFFFF;fontStyle=1;fontSize=22;labelBackgroundColor=none;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-385.5" y="2410.5" width="40" height="38" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="7OVz4w7n9kzEPWy-4BBC-16" value="First step is to process data to make it ready for batch ML inference. Considering that batch ML inference requires big data and real-time predictions are unnecessary Lambda functions will not be suitable for this task due to their limitations in scalability for long running tasks. Hence to overcome this issue AWS Glue Job service is used, but before enabling Glue Job to interact with the data, we need to setup AWS Glue Catalog which is going to define data source and can be populated based on schema of our dataset by Glue Crawler. Once we get and transform data on AWS Glue Job it can be passed to the next processing zone in the data lake. Finally, AWS Glue is configured to&amp;nbsp;orchestrate the process and act based on schedule and/or event driven triggers.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=-4;fontSize=14;labelBackgroundColor=none;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-335" y="1252" width="450" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="7OVz4w7n9kzEPWy-4BBC-17" value="Use AWS Glue to further process and partition your data to help Data Wrangler load data in parallel rather than as a monolithic dataset. Again use AWS Glue catalog as intermediate step between AWS SageMaker Data Wrangler and AWS Glue in order to access metadata for faster loading. After performing feature engineering, aggregations, and other transformations directly within Data Wrangler we export transformed features to SageMaker Feature Store for further consumption by ML models. Finally Feature store is controled and monitored by Data Scientists." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=-4;fontSize=14;labelBackgroundColor=none;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-334" y="1598" width="454" height="152" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="7OVz4w7n9kzEPWy-4BBC-18" value="Loading and preprocessing features from the SageMaker Feature Store for Training Pipeline is done via AWS Glue to ensure batched scalability. Process is orchestrated by AWS Step Function, which based on the specified rules periodically trains 4.1 (or retrains 4.2 if anomalies with data e.g data drift) AI models that later will be deployed to Model Registry. AWS Glue Catalog plays indexing role AWS Glue and SageMaker Training Job for Model 1. All Training Jobs for all models are orchestrated by AWS Step Function. Data from Training Job for Model 2 and 3 are aggregated using Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler in a batched mode and further passed to next Training Job. Version of the models controlled using Git VCS where new models stored in deployed using CI/CD pipelines upon engineers request.&amp;nbsp;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=-4;fontSize=14;labelBackgroundColor=none;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-331" y="1760" width="450" height="210" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="7OVz4w7n9kzEPWy-4BBC-19" value="AI inference is performed by SageMaker real-time endpoint for the deployed ML model. This endpoint pulls history feature values from the batched feature store along with received new features and uses them for real-time predictions on API requests. Internally it uses Elastic Load Balancer and Auto Scaling for QoS. Finally, SageMaker real-time endpoint is logged and monitored via Amazon CloudWatch to track request latency, errors, and invocation metrics, and to detect data drift and anomalies." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=-4;fontSize=14;labelBackgroundColor=none;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-329" y="2260" width="449" height="143.5" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="7OVz4w7n9kzEPWy-4BBC-20" value="The final trained model (Model 5) is registered in SageMaker Model Registry to enable version tracking, evaluation, and deployment.&amp;nbsp; Amazon EventBridge is subscribed to Registery and once there is a new model AWS Lambda is invoked and model is deployed to the Inference endpoint.&amp;nbsp;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=-4;fontSize=14;labelBackgroundColor=none;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-331.5" y="1980.13" width="449" height="105.75" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="UEzPUAAOIrF-is8g5C7q-38" value="Once the inference result is generated, EventBridge rule triggers Lambda function each time a new prediction is made. Lambda function is used to capture the model’s response perform post-processing and store it in S3.&amp;nbsp;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=-4;fontSize=14;labelBackgroundColor=none;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-329" y="2410.5" width="449" height="79.5" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="MtDMNq1w1HRKt9dQc9m3-86" value="&lt;span&gt;On the transformed and cleaned data source that is ready to be visualized we add yet another Glue catalog in order to enable Amazon Athena (the serverless compute engine) that performs queries on the data. The commands are sent from Amazon QuickSight dashboard to Athena to perform queries on our data. End users are able to login QuickSight and use created dashboard to see underlying data and interpret it visually.&lt;/span&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=-4;fontSize=14;labelBackgroundColor=none;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-333.5" y="1467" width="449" height="131" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="MtDMNq1w1HRKt9dQc9m3-99" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=1;jettySize=auto;html=1;movable=1;resizable=1;rotatable=1;deletable=1;editable=1;locked=0;connectable=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="MtDMNq1w1HRKt9dQc9m3-94" target="MtDMNq1w1HRKt9dQc9m3-97">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
